  <p class="answer-label">Answers</p>
  <h1 class="font-display">Urge surfing for binge eating</h1>

  <p class="answer-lead">
    Urge surfing is a technique from DBT (Dialectical Behavior Therapy) that is used in
    evidence-based treatment for binge eating. Instead of fighting an urge, you ride it like a
    wave — noticing it rise, peak, and naturally fall, usually within about 20 to 30 minutes.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Why fighting backfires.</strong> Most of us meet an urge with white knuckles:
    <em>don't think about it, don't feel it, make it stop.</em> But wrestling an urge feeds it
    attention, and attention is what it eats. The harder you push it away, the louder it argues
    back. Urge surfing starts from a different idea: the urge is a wave, and a wave doesn't need
    to be defeated. It needs to be outlasted.
  </p>

  <p><strong>What surfing actually looks like.</strong> Not mystical, not complicated:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Notice where it lives in your body.</strong> Chest, throat, hands, jaw? An urge is
      physical before it's a decision. Finding it in the body turns it from a command into a
      sensation.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Describe it like weather.</strong> "It's building." "It's peaking." "It's a little
      quieter than five minutes ago." You're the person watching the storm from the window, not
      the person inside it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Breathe through the peak.</strong> The peak is the hardest part and also the
      shortest. One slow breath out at a time — the peak passes whether or not you obey it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Let it leave without a ceremony.</strong> Urges fade undramatically. One moment you
      notice it's just… smaller. That's the whole win. Take it.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    <strong>Honest limits.</strong> Urge surfing is a skill, not a switch. The first waves you
    surf will be messy, and some waves are just bigger than others. It gets easier with practice
    — and a wave that wins today doesn't erase the ones you've already ridden. Relapse is a
    normal and expected part of recovery, not a failure.
  </p>

  <h2 class="font-display">Common questions</h2>

  <h3>Does urge surfing work for binge eating?</h3>
  <p>
    Urge surfing is a technique from DBT (Dialectical Behavior Therapy) that is used in
    evidence-based treatment for binge eating. It isn't magic and it isn't instant — it's a skill
    that grows with practice. The core idea it relies on is well established: urges rise, peak,
    and fall on their own, usually within about 20 to 30 minutes.
  </p>

  <h3>How long do you have to surf an urge?</h3>
  <p>
    Usually about 20 to 30 minutes from rise to fall — often less once the peak passes. You're
    not signing up to resist forever; you're staying with one wave until it goes back out.
  </p>

